#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-calibration.rst:7
#: fa66ef93cb6d44a09d7fae49bbf37b25
msgid "ESC Calibration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-calibration.rst:9
#: 901051b50b4244e18959cd499bae3eda
msgid "First, make sure that the correct ESC protocol has been setup. Find your ESC(s) type under :ref:`common-escs-and-motors`, and follow its configuration information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-calibration.rst:11
#: 748bd25f16484ef69827f32161b0d32c
msgid "Vehicles using a traditional PWM controlled ESC (electronic speed controller) for motor output will likely need to be calibrated. **If you are using a digital esc protocol like DShot or are using CAN ESCs then you don't need to calibrate them.**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-calibration.rst:14
#: 3748c6a4303f4f448e2def32903a8ec9
msgid "Remove the propeller from Planes before starting ESC calibration!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-calibration.rst:17
#: 55429bc9366d4ca7a0dfcb0bc422e53f
msgid "Calibrating an ESC involves teaching the ESC what range of throttle inputs it should respond to. It needs to know what PWM value on the throttle channel corresponds with the commanding the motor to be off, and what PWM value corresponds with full throttle. These values are stored inside the ESC."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-calibration.rst:23
#: 193dc3a6478c45719807220efad90244
msgid "You should not try to calibrate your ESC until you have completed both your :ref:`RC inputs <rc-throw-trim>` calibration and your :ref:`servo setup <servo-functions>`. Mission Planner's motor calibration does not work on traditional planes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-calibration.rst:30
#: 7e7e860f48da4f0f81f3d900ca1ca486
msgid "Typical ESC Calibration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-calibration.rst:32
#: 8d64b77e2c634b0f8810de908a1f4c05
msgid "The most common type of ESC calibration for small electric aircraft is the max-throttle/min-throttle method."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-calibration.rst:35
#: ec228cf9887744958da0b790c13c3272
msgid "To perform this type of calibration you will need to be able to power to your motor/escs separately from power to the autopilot. Normally this is easily accomplished by not conneectting the avehicle's main battery and just powering the autopilot via its USB connection. The steps for calibration are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-calibration.rst:39
#: 90056812cf6d4640ae80a76961b5fe1b
msgid "start with the autopilot powered via USB, but with the ESC unpowered (normally just by leaving the flight battery disconnected) and propeller, if a Plane, removed"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-calibration.rst:41
#: 9d399f2bd36a4d88b1b2f201fc021918
msgid "switch to MANUAL flight/control mode"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-calibration.rst:42
#: 4e2da122aeda4221889683eec0baec6e
msgid "disable the safety switch (if fitted) and arm the vehicle"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-calibration.rst:43
#: 4045bb2ba2594318abb273728f4064bf
msgid "move the throttle stick on your transmitter to maximum"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-calibration.rst:44
#: 7077ea77b3ed45ae82227aa69576d3a6
msgid "add power to the ESC by connecting the flight battery"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-calibration.rst:46
#: 12b34033bc3443c39968850cc64b3ae7
msgid "At this stage the ESC/motor should beep to indicate that it is in ESC calibration mode. Typically it will be 2 or 3 quick beeps, but the motor won't turn."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-calibration.rst:50
#: c7e5dd61e4054dadbab7093a43f69fbb
msgid "now lower the throttle stick rapidly to zero"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-calibration.rst:51
#: d783c8abb7714c3b9eec7e3c1b5cc2c5
msgid "the ESC should beep to indicate it has accepted the new calibration range"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-calibration.rst:53
#: f39367c505634bc9a6e2c8ad471f7a95
msgid "now slowly raise the throttle and check that the motor responds correctly"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-calibration.rst:57
#: 078cec244b6e48ea84cb26c6578e438c
msgid "Alternative ESC Calibration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-calibration.rst:59
#: 3dd07669941342a1b1e2ec9f9429cc2b
msgid "If the typical ESC calibration method above doesn't work, instead adjust the SERVOx_MIN and SERVOx_MAX values on the appropriate servo channel (SERVOx) you had set as the Throttle channel in :ref:`servo setup <servo-functions>` to match the existing range of your ESC."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-calibration.rst:63
#: 995cfb294f814a91b45c13f6b46a46cc
msgid "To use this method slowly raise the SERVOx_MIN value until it is set to a value just below the point that the motor comes on. A value about 20 PWM points below the point where the motor starts turning is usually a good choice."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-calibration.rst:68
#: ad81c004a5754bcc9bc9c259cf1d199c
msgid "Next move the transmitter stick to full throttle and adjust SERVOx_MAX until maximum RPM is reached. You can use a tachometer, or just use the sound of the motor as a guide."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-calibration.rst:73
#: 3fd99914df544a128ba3d2171ead7c0e
msgid "Reversing PWM based ESCs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-calibration.rst:75
#: 7cda574b36504aa4b5fb20761b77ea74
msgid "If you are using a reversing ESC for reverse thrust for Plane( see :ref:`Automatic Landing<automatic-landing>` section on Reverse Thrust Landing ) or normal Rovers/Subs, and its PWM based rather than CAN, OneShot, or DShot, then you may need to calibrate it for minimum and maximum PWM values. Sometimes, a button is provided for setting full forward, neutral, and full reverse. In this case, you will need to setup an RCx_OPTION on an RC channel to allow setting full reverse manually. Follow the manufacturers instructions for calibration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-calibration.rst:77
#: 9d8c32351cd64c91992dacc388fe62a1
msgid "Some ESCs just fix the calibration, in which case you may need to adjust the servo/motor's output trim position to be slightly different than 1500 us to obtain zero thrust."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-calibration.rst:79
#: a3dd8ab8ad914a779f994c549ee209ac
msgid "Copter/Sub MOTORx output's trim position cannot be changed, its always 1500us for bi-directional and 1000us for uni-directional motors. Plane and Rover's Throttle output's can have their ``SERVOx_MIN/MAX/TRIM`` values changed as required."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-calibration.rst:81
#: 152170b4cd574250b4bf604353a7372f
msgid "BLHeli ESCs can be set for bi-directional operation using the \"ROTATION\" parameter. Usually they default to full reverse at 1000 us, full forward at 2000 us, and zero at 1500us, but these can be adjusted using the appropriate BLHeli suite application."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-calibration.rst:83
#: ee39ee6f305f4888b9144724a14dc8ec
msgid "AM32 ESCs have similar settings."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-calibration.rst:86
#: 3d8179d73af74f2db16e06206b8adb8f
msgid "Brushed Motor ESCS"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-calibration.rst:88
#: 8f8774d72e394f56974be84edae1288b
msgid "Most brushed motor ESCs have a fixed calibration which will require manually setting the throttle output's ``SERVOx_MAX``, and ``SERVOx_MIN`` parameters to match the ESCs maximum points. ``SERVOx_TRIM`` would be set so that the motor is stopped, if using a reversing ESC."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-calibration.rst:91
#: 347ec360f4564121b2deed0c6a6bf375
msgid "Other ESC Protocols"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-calibration.rst:93
#: b1b01c13941b45ec9126ce29f2c5f75c
msgid "New ESC's using protocols other than PWM often do not require calibration. Some examples include CAN and DShot. The digital signals replace the need for PWM calibration and are usually pre-set to a range of 1000us to 2000us."
msgstr ""
